# Line endings, pinned where they change what gets built.
#
# `frontend/dist` is committed and is what the app serves, and CI rebuilds it
# and fails on any diff. That check is only meaningful if the build is
# byte-identical on every platform — and it was not.
#
# Vite builds `dist/index.html` from `frontend/index.html`, passing the lines
# it does not touch straight through. On a Windows runner git checks the source
# out with CRLF (core.autocrlf defaults to true in Git for Windows), so the
# output came out with CRLF on 13 of its 15 lines while the committed copy had
# LF. The step then reported "frontend/dist is stale" on 2026-09-18 with the
# bundle's own content-hashed asset names matching exactly — the build was
# identical, the line endings were not.
#
# Reproduced by converting the source to CRLF locally and rebuilding: 13
# insertions, 13 deletions, the same stat CI printed.
#
# So the HTML template is checked out with LF everywhere, and the committed
# bundle is never converted on the way in or out.
frontend/index.html text eol=lf

# The build output is stored and checked out byte for byte, never converted.
# `-text` rather than `text eol=lf`: dist holds a PNG as well as HTML/JS/CSS,
# and marking that as text silently rewrote it (3770 -> 3769 bytes) the first
# time this file was written. A build artefact has no line-ending policy; it
# has bytes.
frontend/dist/** -text

# Everything else keeps git's own detection. A repo-wide `* text=auto eol=lf`
# would rewrite every working tree on the next checkout, which is a much bigger
# change than the problem needs and not one to make while a port is in review.
